  "grid_sensitivity_diagnostic": {
    "note": "Checked this session, not part of F208's original methodology: electron_cloud_hartree's uniform radial grid (fixed N at fixed r_max(Z)) under-resolves the 1s core as Z grows, because r_max scales as ~12*(1+sqrt(Z)) while the natural core length scale shrinks as ~1/Z, so grid spacing h=r_max/N grows relative to the core radius. Quantified by rerunning four representative Z at N=1200 (vs. the N=900 used throughout this finding's and F208's own tables) with identical mix=0.2, max_iter=150. The valence-IE shift and especially the 1s-core relativistic shift both move by an amount that GROWS with Z -- comparable to or exceeding the Hartree/no-exchange physical error by Z~30. The qualitative conclusion (severe underbinding vs NIST, dominated by missing exchange-correlation, NOT relativity, which stays sub-meV at both grids) is unchanged at N=1200, but the precise error percentages in the Z=21-30 table above should be read as N=900-grid values with an unquantified additional numerical uncertainty that grows with Z -- NOT as grid-converged physical results. No attempt was made this session to reach grid convergence (would need a non-uniform/log radial grid or N well beyond what fits the sandbox's per-call time budget for an SCF iteration at these Z); this is named as the concrete next step, alongside F208's own named exchange-correlation gap.",
